=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** BGP ECMP configuration — `maximum-paths` is absent from the `address-family ipv4 unicast` block on both leaf routers.

**Mechanism:** In FRR, installing multiple equal-cost BGP paths into the forwarding table (ECMP) requires an explicit `maximum-paths <N>` directive inside the address-family. Without it, FRR selects a single best path via the standard BGP decision process and installs only that one. On both leaf routers the tiebreaker falls to the lowest Router-ID, which belongs to `spine_router_0_0` (172.16.0.1 vs 172.16.0.3). Every packet destined for the remote rack — in both directions — is therefore forced through `spine_router_0_0` exclusively, even though the BGP RIB on each leaf router holds two valid, established paths (one per spine).

**Localization:** `leaf_router_0_0` (ASN 65200) and `leaf_router_0_1` (ASN 65201) — both are missing `maximum-paths 2` under `router bgp … / address-family ipv4 unicast`. `spine_router_0_1` is correctly peered and advertising routes; the fault is purely in how the leaf routers consume those routes.

**Root cause:** BGP ECMP is not enabled on either leaf router; all cross-rack traffic is pinned to a single spine (`spine_router_0_0`), which becomes a single point of failure. When that spine experienced its outage, the leaf routers had no alternate FIB path to fall back on immediately, causing the complete blackout the user reported.

**Fix:** On both `leaf_router_0_0` and `leaf_router_0_1`, add `maximum-paths 2` (and optionally `maximum-paths ibgp 2`) inside `address-family ipv4 unicast`. After `clear ip bgp * soft`, FRR will install both spine paths as ECMP next-hops, distributing cross-rack traffic across both spines and eliminating the single-spine dependency.
=== END ===